title: test_tts_interrupt subprocess/pidfd tests flake under full-suite CPU load
updated_at: "2026-08-22T21:57:04Z"
---
Three different tests in tests/test_tts_interrupt.py failed across four full-suite runs on 2026-08-23, each passing alone and with its whole file (268 passed, 14 skipped):

- test_portable_result_eof_retains_authority_through_supervisor_atexit
- test_direct_children_open_publishes_before_delivered_sigint
- test_outer_pidfd_is_owned_before_delivered_sigint

They spawn real subprocesses and wait on short budgets (e.g. proc.communicate(timeout=2.0) at tests/test_tts_interrupt.py:2658) plus pidfd ownership races, so they lose under concurrent CPU load.

Not caused by any src change: reproduced on master both before and after the arch/compact-once merge, and the same candidate-1 code produced a fully green run (6042 passed, 102 skipped) in its own worktree.

Fix direction: make the waits condition-based rather than fixed short timeouts, or widen the budgets when the machine is loaded. Do not paper over it with retries.

Distinct from a second, environmental failure mode seen the same day: a fresh git worktree venv has only runtime deps, so `uv run pytest` falls back to a system interpreter and the `[sys.executable, "-m", "hark.tts_worker"]` spawn at src/hark/tts_worker.py:1159 cannot import hark. `uv sync --extra dev` in the worktree fixes that one.


## 2026-08-23 follow-up: two distinct mechanisms, not one

Investigation while landing the architecture-review candidates separated the variance into two independent causes. Do not treat them as one bug.

**(a) 14 hasattr-gated pidfd tests flip between run and skip.** `os.pidfd_open` does NOT exist in this uv-managed CPython 3.13.11 (`python -c "import os; print(hasattr(os,'pidfd_open'))"` -> False), so `@pytest.mark.skipif(not hasattr(os, "pidfd_open"))` at tests/test_tts_interrupt.py:1523,1566 should always skip. `skipif` is evaluated per item at SETUP, and tests/test_ask_interrupt.py:1315 installs `pidfd_open` onto `capture_mod.os` (the shared `os` module) with `raising=False`. Whether that attribute is present when a later item's setup runs decides run-vs-skip. Evidence: the same tree gave `6053 passed / 88 skipped` and `6040 passed / 102 skipped` on consecutive full runs — a delta of exactly 14. NOTE: running `test_ask_interrupt.py` then `test_tts_interrupt.py` in one process does NOT reproduce it (324 passed, 14 skipped), so the leak trigger is some other file or a fixture, not that simple ordering. Still to be pinned.

**(b) Load-sensitive subprocess budgets.** Separate from (a). `test_outer_pidfd_is_owned_before_delivered_sigint` is gated on `sys.platform` only (line 1428), always runs, passes standalone in 0.15s, and failed only in a full suite under concurrent CPU load. Same family: `test_tts_play_queue.py::test_claim_lock_timeout_reports_owner_and_queue` failed once with `subprocess.TimeoutExpired ... after 2.0 seconds` and passes standalone (30 passed).

**(c) Not a mechanism, but a trap that looks like one.** A fresh git worktree venv holds only runtime deps, so `uv run pytest` falls back to a system interpreter and the `[sys.executable, "-m", "hark.tts_worker"]` spawn at src/hark/tts_worker.py:1159 cannot import hark. Fix with `uv sync --extra dev` in the worktree. Also note a fresh worktree venv lacks the `wake` / `smart-turn` extras that the main checkout has, which shifts skip counts by ~28 — so never compare skip totals across checkouts.


## 2026-08-23: third instance, signal-handling timing

`tests/test_ask_interrupt.py::test_capture_sigint_aborts_stream_and_restores_handlers` fails roughly 1 in 12 runs when run as a single item, measured 12x on two different trees: master (1/12) and the arch/audio-seams refactor branch (1/12). Equal rates, so it is NOT caused by the frame-source refactor — it is another pre-existing timing flake, this one in SIGINT delivery / handler restoration rather than a subprocess budget.

Method note for whoever picks this up: single runs mislead here. The first probe showed "fails on the branch, passes on master" and reversed on re-run; only a repeat count separated them. Measure a rate, do not compare one run to one run.

2026-08-23 (later): a FOURTH test in the same file joins the set —
test_long_lived_caller_delegates_next_sigint_after_synth_finishes
(tests/test_tts_interrupt.py:5148). Failed once in a full-suite run on the
arch/audio-seams branch, then 0/15 in isolation on that branch AND 0/15 in
isolation on master, so it is the same load-dependent subprocess race, not a
regression from the audio work. Ruled out the one plausible causal path: the
branch changed mic_mute's TTS hold to a MuteHold lease, but src/hark/speech.py
is byte-identical across the merge and this test calls run_tts(play=False), so
the mute path is never entered.

Its specific race is visible in the child script: it prints READY_AGAIN
(:5181) BEFORE entering the try/except KeyboardInterrupt (:5182-5186), and the
parent sends the second SIGINT as soon as it reads that line (:5202). A SIGINT
landing in that window raises KeyboardInterrupt outside the handler, so the
child dies with a traceback and the assert on returncode == 0 (:5208) fails.
Same shape as the others: the signal is sent relative to a stdout marker rather
than to a state the child has actually reached.

Method note (repeat of the lesson in the entry above): single runs mislead here.
Every claim in this entry is 15 runs per tree.

2026-08-23 (post-merge, master @ ee6e541): the family is WIDER than this bug's
title says — it is not confined to tests/test_tts_interrupt.py.

Two back-to-back full-suite runs on the same checkout, same commit, 2 failed
each, with **disjoint** failure sets:

  run 1: test_ask_interrupt.py::test_signal_after_worker_start_upgrades_before_native_cleanup
         test_tts_interrupt.py::test_cli_pending_sigint_during_boundary_install_is_typed_and_traceback_free
  run 2: test_tts_interrupt.py::test_long_lived_caller_delegates_next_sigint_after_synth_finishes
         test_tts_interrupt.py::test_pipe_creation_publishes_both_fds_before_delivered_sigint

Zero overlap between runs is the signature of a load race, not a regression: a
regression fails the same test every time. Seven distinct tests have now been
seen failing across all runs, spanning BOTH interrupt test files. Retitle to
cover test_ask_interrupt.py as well.

Rate, measured on this machine, main checkout: pre-merge master (216s) 1 failed;
post-merge master (295s, 305s) 2 failed, 2 failed. The rate tracks suite
duration, which is the expected shape if the cause is CPU contention — the
longer the run, the more chances each signal race has to lose. 30s of that
duration was one test spending real wall clock on the discard cap; fixed in
b8ea44b, so re-measure the rate against the shorter suite before assuming the
merge itself raised it.

Practical consequence worth stating plainly: the full suite does not currently
go green on an unloaded run of this machine either. Anyone treating "0 failed"
as the merge gate will be blocked by this bug, not by their own change.

LOAD ATTRIBUTION EVENT, 2026-08-23 (coordinator). A full-suite run on master immediately after the B186 merge produced SIX failures at once, all in tests/test_tts_interrupt.py: test_pre_submit_interrupt_does_not_strand_false_running_state, test_public_interrupt_is_bounded_when_procfs_fails_after_payload_spawn, test_procfs_loss_with_adopted_descendant_is_bounded_and_orphan_free, test_already_exited_payload_still_cleans_adopted_descendant, test_pipe_creation_publishes_both_fds_before_delivered_sigint, test_outer_pidfd_is_owned_before_delivered_sigint. Result line: 6 failed, 6211 passed, 74 skipped in 348.79s.

Machine state during that run: load average 199, FOUR concurrent pytest processes from other agent worktrees. This is the largest simultaneous failure count recorded for this family so far, and it lines up with the heaviest concurrent load recorded so far. Two new family members here (pre_submit_interrupt, pipe_creation_publishes_both_fds) bring the known count to at least 11 distinct tests.

Ruled out B186 (the merge that immediately preceded the run) with a controlled experiment rather than by assumption:
1. tests/test_tts_interrupt.py contains zero references to record_turn / _emit_turn / transcripts, so it never reaches the new code.
2. 8 isolated runs of the one test that still failed alone: transcript log ON = 7 pass / 1 fail; transcript log OFF via HARK_NO_TRANSCRIPT_LOG=1 (which disables B186 new file write in the ask path entirely) = 6 pass / 2 fail. Disabling the new write did not help and if anything hurt, so the new write is not the mechanism.
3. 5 of the 6 passed in isolation immediately after the full run.

Also note for whoever fixes this: one failure mode in this batch is NOT a timeout. tests/test_tts_interrupt.py:7588 failed with "DID NOT RAISE OSError", and :5271 with an assertion on Popen returncode -2 (SIGINT). So widening timeouts alone will not fix the family - some members are asserting on a specific signal/errno outcome that load reorders, which is further evidence for the marker-vs-state root cause rather than for a budget that is merely too tight.

NEW CANDIDATE, second independent sighting 2026-08-23: tests/test_dashboard_server.py::test_rest_to_stream_handoff_captures_rotation_after_snapshot. Reported independently by the B186 agent and the B187/B177 agent, on different branches and different runs. Passes alone and passes with its whole 66-test file. Neither agent could have caused it: it references neither hark.cli / hark.transcripts (B186) nor provider / present_for_monitor (B187/B177).

Significant because it is the FIRST candidate outside the interrupt files. If the root cause is marker-vs-state signalling in the interrupt harness, this one should NOT be in the family - a threaded SSE handoff race is a different mechanism that merely shares the trigger (load). Treat it as a separate bug that happens to have the same precondition, and do not let a fix for the interrupt family be judged on whether it fixes this. Worth its own bl item once the interrupt family is closed.

Count now: at least 11 in test_tts_interrupt.py / test_ask_interrupt.py, plus test_ask_capture_deadline.py (one sighting) and this dashboard test (two sightings).

## 2026-08-23: FOUR mechanisms, three fixed, on branch fix/B184-interrupt-flake

Root-caused with deterministic, zero-CPU-load reproductions. No load generator
is needed for any of this; earlier "under load" timings on this box were
contaminated anyway (dead sshfs mount, ~128 threads in D-state).

**(1) Ambient SIGINT disposition. FIXED.** `os.kill(os.getpid(), SIGINT)` only
becomes a KeyboardInterrupt if something raises for it, and the tests never
established that. Two ways it breaks: a suite launched as a shell background job
or under `nohup` inherits SIGINT=SIG_IGN and the kernel discards the signal
outright; and `hark.lifecycle.install_signal_handlers` (src/hark/lifecycle.py:150)
has no uninstall, so the first test that runs an ambient loop leaves its
`_stop_handler` -- which only records a shutdown request and returns -- owning
SIGINT/SIGTERM for the rest of the session. Attribution evidence: a per-test
leak detector showed the handler flip to
`<function install_signal_handlers.<locals>._stop_handler>` at
tests/test_config_watch.py::test_ambient_loop_file_watch_reload and stay there
for the following ~5300 teardowns. test_tts_interrupt.py sorts after that file;
test_ask_interrupt.py sorts before it, which is why the ask members needed the
launcher case instead.

REPRO (100% deterministic): run the target test with SIGINT inherited as
SIG_IGN:

    python -c "import signal,subprocess,sys; signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, signal.SIG_IGN); sys.exit(subprocess.run(sys.argv[1:]).returncode)" \
      .venv/bin/python -m pytest -q "tests/test_ask_interrupt.py::test_signal_after_capture_state_publication_restores_global"

On master that fails every time with the exact message seen in full runs:
`Failed: signal after capture-state publication reached body`. With the fix it
passes, and the whole file passes 56/56 under the same launcher.

FIX: tests/conftest.py pins SIGINT per test and restores SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGHUP
plus `lifecycle._handlers_installed`, so every test gets the signal environment
it gets when run alone.

**(2) A blocked/pending SIGINT carried across tests. FIXED (defensively).**
`SigintMaskGuard` records the mask it finds on entry and restores exactly that.
Correct nesting -- but a SIGINT already blocked when a guarded region starts is
still blocked when it ends, so a signal planted inside is never delivered and is
left pending for whichever later code unblocks it. Probe, control vs treatment,
100% separation:

    SIGINT not blocked on entry -> raised = KeyboardInterrupt, fd closed
    SIGINT already blocked      -> raised = None; after: blocked=True pending=True

That second line is both a "DID NOT RAISE KeyboardInterrupt" failure and the
source of strays: KeyboardInterrupt surfacing in an unrelated frame and aborting
the session. Observed on master in json/decoder.py and _pytest/_code/code.py,
and once mid-way through test_tts_interrupt.py.

FIX: conftest drains a blocked/pending SIGINT at both edges of every test
(unblock under SIG_IGN, so the kernel drops it and the drain cannot raise).

HONEST LIMIT: this removes the precondition, but the leak detector never caught
SIGINT blocked at a real test boundary, so the precondition is not shown to
arise naturally. Guards left for `__del__` (restore_suppressing keeps `_active`
set, B185) are the plausible route, which makes B185 implicated here.

**(3) Markers published before the state they advertise. FIXED.** Three child
scripts printed READY_SECOND/READY_AGAIN *before* entering the try whose
`except KeyboardInterrupt` the parent's next signal targets, so a signal landing
in the gap killed the child (returncode -2). Markers now print from inside the
try. Covers test_long_lived_caller_delegates_next_sigint_after_synth_finishes
and test_pre_submit_interrupt_does_not_strand_false_running_state (both were the
`-2` failures) and _library_hung_tts_child.

**(4) fstat on a bare fd number races fd reuse. NOT FIXED -- this is the
"DID NOT RAISE OSError" family, and mechanism (1) does NOT explain it.**
`with pytest.raises(OSError): os.fstat(fd)` is not a safe closure assertion:
POSIX hands out the lowest free descriptor, so any unrelated open() in the
process reclaims the number just closed and fstat succeeds. Deterministic
proof, no load:

    r, w = os.pipe(); os.close(r); os.close(w)
    keep = open("/dev/null")          # takes the lowest free fd == r
    os.fstat(r)                       # SUCCEEDS -> "DID NOT RAISE OSError"

Observed output: `fd 3: fstat SUCCEEDED after close`, `fd 4: closed (9)`.

RECOMMENDED FIX: assert identity, not validity -- capture (st_dev, st_ino) when
the fd is created and treat "invalid OR different inode" as released. Several
tests in this file already do exactly that (see the `source_stat` /
`original_inode` / `foreign_inode` sites), so the technique is established here;
roughly 70 `os.fstat` call sites would need auditing. Left undone deliberately:
a 70-site sweep with no on-demand in-suite reproduction is not something to land
unverified.

Also unfixed, and separate: mechanism (a) from the earlier entry. Confirmed that
`os.pidfd_open` really is absent in this interpreter (uv-managed CPython
3.13.11, python-build-standalone/Clang), so hark's production pidfd path never
runs here at all, and the tests that install a fake `pidfd_open` onto the shared
`os` module still flip other tests' skipif between run and skip.

Budget changes made as well: both interrupt files now take one `_WAIT_S` hang
guard (30s, HARK_TEST_WAIT_S to override) in place of inline 1-5s caps, with the
single wait that proves something does NOT finish named `_HANG_PROOF_S`.
Child-script budgets are deliberately untouched -- child source lives in string
literals and cannot see module constants (getting this wrong is a NameError in
the child that reads as five unrelated test failures).

